  <content>
  <section><title>Factoring Perfect Square Trinomials</title>
<para>To factor <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">perfect square trinomial</term>s, follow the formulas:</para>
<para>\(a^2+2ab+b^2=(a+b)^2\)</para>
<para>\(a^2-2ab+b^2=(a-b)^2\)</para>
<para>Let's look at an example of how to factor perfect square trinomials.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example</emphasis></para>
<para>Factor \(9x^2+12x+4\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Does the trinomial fit the perfect square trinomial pattern, \(a^2+2ab+b^2\)? If so, write the first term as \(a^2\) and last constant as \(b^2\).</para>
<list>
  <item> Is the first term a perfect square? Write it as a square, \(a^2\). 
    Is \(9x^2\) a perfect square? 
    Yes — write it as \((3x)^2\). 
    \(\begin{array}{l}9x^2\;+\;12x\;+\;4 \\ (3x)^2\;\end{array}\) </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> Is the last term a perfect square? Write it as a square, \(b^2\). 
    Is 4 a perfect square? 
    Yes — write it as \((2)^2\). 
    \(\begin{array}{l}9x^2\;+\;12x\;+\;4 \\ (3x)^2\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;(2)^2\end{array}\) </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> Check the middle term. Is it \(2ab\)?
    Is \(12x\) twice the product of \(3x\) and 2? 
    Does it match? 
    Yes, so we have a perfect trinomial. </item>
</list>
<para>\(\begin{array}{c}9x^2\;\;\;\;\;\;+\;\;\;\;\;\;12x\;\;\;\;\;\;+\;\;\;\;\;\;4\; \\ (3x)^2\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;(2)^2 \\ (3x)^2\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;(2)^2 \\ {\style{color:green}\searrow}\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;{\style{color:green}\swarrow} \\ 2(3x)(2) \\ 12x\;\end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Factor the binomial into: \(a^2+2ab+b^2=(a+b)^2\). 
  \(9x^2+12x+4\) 
  \(\begin{array}{l}a^2\;\;\;\;\;\;+2\cdot a\cdot b+b^2 \\ \;({\style{color:red}3}{\style{color:red}x})^2\;+2 \cdot {\style{color:red}3}{\style{color:red}x} \cdot {\style{color:purple}2}+{\style{color:purple}2}^2\; \\ \;\;\;\;\;\;\;(a+b)^2\; \\ \;\;\;\;\;({\style{color:red}3}{\style{color:red}x}+{\style{color:green}2})^{\style{color:green}2}\end{array}\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Check. 
  \((3x+2)^2\) 
  \((3x)^2+2 \cdot 3x \cdot 2+2^2\) 
  \(9x^2+12x+4 ✔\) </para>
<title>Try It: Factoring Perfect Square Trinomials</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Factor \(9x^2-30x+25\).</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to factor a perfect square trinomial:</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Write the first term as \(a^2\) and the last constant as \(b^2\). 
    \(a^2=(3x)^2\), \(b^2=5^2\) </para>
  <para>Make sure the middle term is \(2ab\).
    \(2ab=2(3x)(5)=30x\) </para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Factor the binomial into: \(a^2-2ab+b^2=(a-b)^2\). 
    \((3x-5)^2\) </para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Check. </para>
  <para>\((3x-5)(3x-5)\)
    \(=9x^2-15x-15x+25\) 
    \(=9x^2-30x+25\) </para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
